"On the e-positivity of trees and spiders - ScienceDirect (https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jcta.2022.105608)\nciteturn3search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.3 years ago; Crawled: last week; We show in Theorem 3.4 that the sum of the residues of the leg lengths of an e-positive spider modulo any positive integer must be less than twice that integer. While this theorem is not used in our attempt at Conjecture 1.2, it provides a simple test for e-positivity and rules out the e-positivity of many classes of spiders, which we discuss in Corollary 3.5.\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nIn 1995, Richard Stanley introduced the chromatic symmetric function of a graph - a generalization of the well known chromatic polynomial [22]. Stanley's seminal paper included numerous properties of the chromatic symmetric function and an equivalent form of the Stanley-Stembridge conjecture that the chromatic symmetric function of the incomparability graph of any (3+ 1)-free poset is e-positive, i.e. a nonnegative linear combination of elementary symmetric functions [23]. This conjecture has led to numerous studies related to the classification of graphs with e-positive chromatic symmetric function, or simply e-positive graphs for short [6], [7], [8], [9], [11], [12], [13], [14], [18], [19].\n\nOne difficulty of working with the chromatic symmetric function is that there is no deletion-contraction relation like that of the chromatic polynomial. Many have circumvented this problem, however, by using quasisymmetric functions, functions in noncommuting variables, and a vertex weighted version of the chromatic symmetric function [4], [5], [13], [21]. In each of these cases, the generalization of the chromatic symmetric function allows for some form of a deletion-contraction relation that can then be used to show properties that are inherited by the chromatic symmetric function.\n\nAnother active area of research regards the question of whether the chromatic symmetric function can distinguish trees up to isomorphism [16], [18], [19]. Using a probabilistic algorithm, the answer is known to be affirmative for trees of up to 29 vertices [15]. The affirmative is also known for certain simplified families of trees such as spiders and caterpillars [2], [3], [16], [18].\n\nRecently, Dahlberg, She, and van Willigenburg have combined these two avenues and studied the e-positivity of trees. This study is also in part motivated by Stanley's initial observation that being e-positive is related to being claw-free and that trees are likely e-positive only “by accident”. In line with this observation, Dahlberg, She, and van Willigenburg show that many classes of trees are not e-positive, and exhibit numerous conditions for the e-positivity of trees and graphs with cut vertices. In particular, they give the following theorem and conjecture.\n\nTheorem 1.1\n\n## Dahlberg, She, and van Willigenburg [8]\n\nLet T be an n-vertex tree with a vertex of degree 𝑑 ≥3. If 𝑑 ≥log2⁡𝑛+ 1, then T is not e-positive.\n\nConjecture 1.2\n\n## Dahlberg, She, and van Willigenburg [8]\n\nAny tree with a vertex of degree at least four is not e-positive.\n\nDahlberg, She, and van Willigenburg obtain their results by reducing, in many cases, the e-positivity of trees to that of spiders. Specifically, they show that certain classes of spiders are not e-positive and then use a lemma to extend the same results to some corresponding classes of trees. In this paper, we follow the same strategy and give further conditions for the e-positivity of trees and spiders. Our main result is a partial resolution of Conjecture 1.2 to trees with a vertex of degree at least six, which we obtain via a similar reduction to spiders. Our paper is structured as follows.\n\nIn Section 2 we review necessary background on chromatic symmetric functions and notation that will be used throughout the paper.\n\nIn Section 3 we expand on many of the tests for e-positivity from [8] and build up to our main result. We show in Theorem 3.4 that the sum of the residues of the leg lengths of an e-positive spider modulo any positive integer must be less than twice that integer. While this theorem is not used in our attempt at Conjecture 1.2, it provides a simple test for e-positivity and rules out the e-positivity of many classes of spiders, which we discuss in Corollary 3.5. We then give a more complicated criterion for the e-positivity of spiders that leads to our main result, stated in Theorem 3.10.\n\nTheorem\n\nAny spider with at least six legs is not e-positive.\n\nWe then extend this theorem to trees and graphs with cut vertices by applying [8, Lemmas 12 and 13]. This yields the partial resolution of Conjecture 1.2.\n\nTheorem\n\nAny tree with a vertex of degree at least six is not e-positive.\n\nTheorem\n\nAny graph with a cut vertex whose deletion produces a graph with at least six connected components is not e-positive.\n\nIn Sections 4 and 5, we turn to calculating specific coefficients in the chromatic symmetric functions of spiders and come up with further conditions for e-positivity.\n\nIn Section 4 we focus on spiders with three or four legs. We present a decomposition for the chromatic symmetric function of spiders, in Lemma 4.4, in terms of the chromatic symmetric functions of spiders with fewer legs. By using this decomposition, we are able to calculate specific expansion coefficients of certain elementary symmetric functions. Very recently, Wang and Wang have also used this decomposition to further classify the spiders with three legs that are e-positive [24].\n\nIn Section 5 we apply this decomposition to spiders with more legs. Our main result of this section gives further conditions for when spiders with odd length legs are e-positive. While in [8] it is found that spiders with at least 3 odd length legs are not e-positive, we find that spiders with at least four legs, exactly two odd length legs, and longest leg with even length, are also not e-positive.\n\nFinally, in Section 6 we conclude with questions and conjectures that suggest the e-positivity of spiders is itself an interesting subject.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA quantitative way to e-positivity of trees - arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/paper/2503.09484v2)\nciteturn3search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.6 years ago; Crawled: last month; Li ... Zheng and Tom verified this conjecture for all trees with maximum degree at least 5 and spiders with maximum degree 4, and in their proofs the following necessary condition given by Wolfgang plays an important role: every connected graph having e-positive chromatic symmetric function must contain a connected partition of every type. ... At first, we give an explicit formula for the e-coefficients of trees in terms of their connected partitions, by which e-positivity is equivalent to a series of inequalities for the numbers of connected partitions.\n# arXiv++ Combinatorics\n\nBrowse math.CO papers from arXiv\n\n## A quantitative way to e-positivity of trees\n\nEthan Y. H. Li\n\nPublished: 2025-03-12 | Updated: 2026-02-10\n\nComments: 19 pages, 6 figures\n\nNote:\n\nPublication:\n\nacyclic orientation chromatic symmetric function graph orientation symmetric function connected graph tree partition\n\n# • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\n### Abstract\n\nIn 2020, Dahlberg, She, and van Willigenburg conjectured that the chromatic symmetric function of any tree with maximum degree at least 4 is not e-positive. Zheng and Tom verified this conjecture for all trees with maximum degree at least 5 and spiders with maximum degree 4, and in their proofs the following necessary condition given by Wolfgang plays an important role: every connected graph having e-positive chromatic symmetric function must contain a connected partition of every type. In order to make further progress on this conjecture, we refine Wolfgang's result in a quantitative way. At first, we give an explicit formula for the e-coefficients of trees in terms of their connected partitions, by which e-positivity is equivalent to a series of inequalities for the numbers of connected partitions. Based on this formula, we present several necessary conditions on the numbers of connected partitions or acyclic orientations for trees to be e-positive. These necessary conditions turn out to be characterizations on the structure of e-positive trees, and as sample applications we prove the non-e-positivity of several classes of trees with maximum degree 3 or 4. We further make more discussions and calculations on trees with maximum degree 4 and having a connected partition of every type, which inspire us to come up with a list of open problems towards the final resolution of the above conjecture.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSéminaire Lotharingien de Combinatoire 93B (2025) (https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~slc/wpapers/FPSAC2025/118.pdf)\nciteturn3search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; and Zheng showed [27, Corollary 4.6] that such a spider S is not e-positive. ... ing e-positivity of spiders S(a, b, c) with exactly three legs. ... (a, b, c) ∈{(5, 3, 2), (6, 4, 2), (8, 6, 2), (9, 7, 2), (12, 4, 2), (8, 5, 3), (14, 9, 5)}. ... The author would like to think José Aliste-Prieto, Richard Stanley, Stephanie van Willi-\n9\nNow the remaining Part 3 of Theorem 2.1 follows from Proposition 2.12, Lemma 2.13,\nLemma 2.6, and Lemma 2.7.\nProposition 2.15. If c ≥2 and 2c ≤b ≤c2\n2 , then G is missing a connected partition of some\ntype λ ⊢n.\nWe can also prove that spiders with four legs are not e-positive.\nProof of Theorem 1.8. Because S has four legs, we have c ≥c1 + 1 ≥2. If b ≤c2\n2 , the result\nfollows from Parts 1, 2, and 3 of Theorem 2.1. If b ≥c2\n2 , then\nn ≥2b + c + 1 ≥c2 + c + 1,\nand Zheng showed [27, Corollary 4.6] that such a spider S is not e-positive.\nBecause a spider with two legs is a path and is well-known to be e-positive [20,\nProposition 5.3], the problem of classifying e-positive spiders is now reduced to classify-\ning e-positivity of spiders S(a, b, c) with exactly three legs.\nFor c = 1, there are infinite families of e-positive and non-e-positive spiders of the\nform S(a, b, 1) [8, 23, 25]. Although some necessary and sufficient conditions are known\nin terms of divisibility properties of a and b, Figure 3 suggests that a full classification\nwould be difficult.\nFor c ≥2, we can use Theorem 2.1 to rule out many cases and by calculating specific\ncoefficients, we can check many cases by computer. We conjecture that there are in fact\nonly finitely many e-positive spiders of the form S(a, b, c) where c ≥2.\nConjecture 2.16. Let S = S(a, b, c) be a spider with three legs and suppose that c ≥2. Then S\nis e-positive if and only if\n(a, b, c) ∈{(5, 3, 2), (6, 4, 2), (8, 6, 2), (9, 7, 2), (12, 4, 2), (8, 5, 3), (14, 9, 5)}.\nWe checked Conjecture 2.16 up to n = 95 vertices.\nAcknowledgements\nThe author would like to think José Aliste-Prieto, Richard Stanley, Stephanie van Willi-\ngenburg, and Kai Zheng for helpful discussions.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nChromatic symmetric functions in the elementary basis | SymCat (https://www.symmetricfunctions.com/chromaticEexpansion.htm)\nciteturn3search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; A September 2026 preprint by OpenAI announces a proof of this positivity conjecture [Ope26, Thm. ... For example, [WW23] classifies positivity for all broom graphs and most double broom graphs, and [TWW24] proves \\(\\elementaryE\\)-positivity for the spiders \\(S(4m+2,2m,1). ...   * Some spider and related families are not \\(\\elementaryE\\)-positive; see [FKKM+20] and the later work on trees and connected partitions [Tom26]. ... A quantitative connected-partition approach to further tree obstructions is developed in [Li25].\n\n  * Positive \\(\\elementaryE\\)-expansions are known for KPKP grap